    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_fb974005d77a446ef72bef1afb13d0933b0322ce53980cad290a970eeff832b7",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "Cubic symmetry compresses the superdiffusion tensor to a scalar D, while the radius selects which geometric horizon families enter that scalar. Coordinate horizons contribute in one displayed interval and additional (1,+/-1,0,...) families in the next, so introducing a family changes the combinatorics and can change fixed-r large-d behavior. For fixed d and small r, the source reuses the same horizon sum as for F(t), transferring the displayed zeta(d)-dependent leading term and its O(r^{1/2-delta}) remainder to diffusivity. This shared representation transports both analytic information and its unresolved contour caveat between observables; it does not turn the apparent RH equivalence into a theorem.",
      "parent_sources": [
