    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_469f234762d87bf9c3db3baac89a2cb0f3601c135759e061695c5b8654be2da9",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "When a principal horizon becomes incipient, its width vanishes but it contains countably many horizons one dimension lower, replacing a finite dominant-family problem by a summability problem. In the touching cubic model, rescaled transverse visibility gives each such horizon an asymptotic weight proportional to L^{-5}t^{-2}; summing lattice directions converges through dimension five and diverges from dimension six, supplying the source's mechanism for a critical change in survival decay.",
